#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097882 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$, as shown below. If we rotate this region $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we get a solid of revolution.\n\n![](images/e9/e9902ba13717e576f07a15396e6a78fd823a3ad92762688d7802cc7ac95849c0.png)\n\nWe can compute the volume of the solid using the shell method. We divide the interval from $x=a$ to $x=b$ on the $x$-axis to get vertical slices. Consider one such slice of width $\\Delta{x}$, as shown in the picture below (on the left). When this rectangle is rotated about the $y$-axis, it forms a solid shell (i.e., the skin of a cylinder).\n\n![](images/c7/c7be68342d347b8ef48e3cf2b8e20b88bedca594fc3d4846f03b5391ae8dbfc2.png)\n\nSince we assume that $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, the outer radius of the shell approximately equals the inner radius $x$. The thickness of the shell is $\\Delta{x}$ and its height is $y=f(x)$.\n\nImagine we cut this shell and unfold it to obtain a rectangular solid, as shown below. Its height is $y=f(x)$, its thickness is $\\Delta{x}$, and its length is $2\\pi x$ (the circumference of the shell).\n\n![](images/a4/a40f23b110ab24c55665784219b7013e587487f88d8d7857260458b9c7a20d2d.png)\n\nThe volume of the shell approximately equals the volume of the rectangular solid:\n\n$$\nV_{shell} \\mid \\approx V_{rec}; = 2πx \\cdot y \\cdot Δx; = 2πxf(x)Δx\n$$\n\nThe sum of all the shells will approximate the total volume we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as $\\Delta{x} \\to 0$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{a}^{b}xydx \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{a}^{b}xf(x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a79e6b6c-f1bd-5918-96f5-7a2b9a19608e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5287 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097883 :tutorial/content "Consider the region enclosed by the curves $y=f(x)$ on the top, $y=g(x)$ on the bottom, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$, as shown below. We assume that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq g(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in [a,b]$.\n\n![](images/37/372ece19360ca4f48a5ef48637e49d8f401ee2f1fd562f13c481c767a536bf6c.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we use the shell method as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{a}^{b}x([upper] - [lower])dx \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{a}^{b}x([f(x)] - [g(x)])dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d08e59d8-b71f-516e-8909-6d8ab812bb5c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5291 :tutorial/title "Rotating the Region Between Two Curves About the Y-Axis"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097884 :tutorial/content "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $y = f(x)$ about the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $y$-axis:\n\n![](images/ad/ad563abd1e7f4a413337dc9527d2c3703d4851e756f385a75797c6f5962cf51f.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}xf(x)dx \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}x(2x - 2x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}2x^{2} - 2x^{3}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine the volume using the disc method, we would first have to express our curve as two functions of $y$, one for the right-hand side and one for the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n{x = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1 - 2y},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1 - 2y},\\end{vmatrix}x < \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/ee/ee7ef70a2a280c42ceea9dc6b8bdb3e3bafbfec0256350cbabde287d33d9884b.png)\n\nThe required volume equals the difference between the volumes generated when the right and left curves are rotated about the $y$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left(\\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2y} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2y} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!" :tutorial/id #uuid "ba06c616-2c80-5ffb-a64d-eda65b594b9e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5294 :tutorial/title "Advantages of the Shell Method"}]}
